1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
On November 21, 1915,

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:04,640
a ship called Endurance

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,160
sank in the Weddell Sea,

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,200
near Antarctica.

5
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:08,960
All twenty-eight men

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:09,920
of her expedition

7
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,200
were still alive.

8
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:12,720
But they had no ship,

9
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,880
no radio, and no way

10
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:16,320
to tell the outside world

11
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:17,040
where they were.

12
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:18,480
They were stranded

13
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:19,920
on drifting sea ice,

14
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:21,920
with three small lifeboats

15
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,120
and whatever supplies

16
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,480
they had managed to save.

17
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,160
To get everyone home,

18
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:27,920
six men would eventually

19
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:28,800
have to cross more

20
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,400
than eight hundred miles

21
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,880
of open ocean in a boat barely

22
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,080
seven metres long.

23
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:35,760
The other twenty-two

24
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:36,560
would have to wait,

25
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:38,080
without knowing whether

26
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:39,680
those six had survived.

27
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:41,280
And even if the boat

28
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:42,800
reached help, someone

29
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:44,560
would still have to find a way

30
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:45,680
back through the ice.

31
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:49,120
In 1914, Ernest Shackleton

32
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,560
set out to lead the first

33
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:52,400
expedition across Antarctica,

34
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:53,920
travelling from one coast

35
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:55,040
to the other by way

36
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:56,080
of the South Pole.

37
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:57,120
His ship,

38
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:58,800
Endurance, was supposed

39
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:00,080
to carry the expedition

40
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:01,280
into the Weddell Sea,

41
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:02,240
where the crossing

42
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:02,880
would begin.

43
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,440
But in January 1915,

44
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:06,800
moving pack ice

45
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:08,240
closed around the vessel.

46
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:09,440
For months,

47
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:10,960
Captain Frank Worsley

48
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:12,080
tracked their position

49
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:13,360
while the crew maintained

50
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:14,560
the ship and waited

51
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:15,840
for a chance to escape.

52
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:17,520
The sea ice moved

53
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,960
with the winds and currents.

54
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:20,480
Instead of carrying them

55
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:21,840
toward their destination,

56
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:23,360
it took the trapped ship

57
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:24,640
wherever it drifted.

58
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:26,480
Then, in October,

59
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:27,360
the pressure

60
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:28,480
became too great.

61
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,080
The wooden hull began

62
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:30,880
to give way.

63
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:32,160
Water entered,

64
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:33,920
and on October 27,

65
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:35,600
Shackleton ordered the men

66
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:36,800
to abandon ship.

67
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:38,880
They carried three lifeboats,

68
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:40,480
provisions and equipment

69
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:42,000
onto the surrounding ice.

70
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:43,840
On November 21,

71
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:45,600
Endurance sank.

72
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:46,880
The expedition

73
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:47,920
had never reached

74
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:48,800
its starting point

75
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:50,160
on the Antarctic continent.

76
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:52,480
Now the men had to find

77
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:53,680
another way home.

78
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,000
Shackleton first tried

79
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:57,760
moving toward land on foot.

80
00:01:58,080 --> 00:01:59,680
But hauling heavy lifeboats

81
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:00,800
across broken ice

82
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:02,480
proved almost impossible.

83
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:04,480
Ridges blocked their path,

84
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:06,240
cracks forced detours,

85
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:07,040
and the men

86
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:08,320
made little progress.

87
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,080
They abandoned the march

88
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:11,360
and established a camp

89
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:12,480
on a drifting floe.

90
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:13,760
For months,

91
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,360
they waited as the current

92
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:16,320
carried them north.

93
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:17,920
They rationed their food,

94
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:19,840
hunted seals and penguins,

95
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:21,520
and kept the lifeboats ready.

96
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:23,920
In April 1916,

97
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:25,200
the ice beneath them

98
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:26,560
began breaking apart.

99
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:28,640
On April 9, they launched

100
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,000
their boats into the water.

101
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:31,680
For nearly a week,

102
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,440
the crews rowed and sailed

103
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:34,960
between moving floes,

104
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:36,160
struggling to reach

105
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:37,120
Elephant Island.

106
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:38,640
On April 15,

107
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:40,400
all twenty-eight men

108
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:41,440
finally landed.

109
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:42,560
They had reached

110
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:43,440
solid ground,

111
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:44,960
but Elephant Island

112
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:46,720
was uninhabited and far

113
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:48,080
from normal shipping routes.

114
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:49,920
No rescue ship knew

115
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:50,560
they were there.

116
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:52,240
Shackleton decided

117
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:54,080
that a small crew must leave

118
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:54,800
to find help.

119
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:56,160
Their destination

120
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:57,280
was South Georgia,

121
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:00,613
roughly 1,300 kilometres —

122
00:03:00,747 --> 00:03:02,107
over 800 miles —

123
00:03:02,133 --> 00:03:02,960
to the northeast,

124
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:04,640
across the Scotia Sea.

125
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:06,240
There were whaling stations

126
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:06,960
on the island.

127
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:08,560
If the men could reach one,

128
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:09,440
they could obtain

129
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,280
help and organise a rescue.

130
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:12,720
Their only suitable

131
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:14,160
vessel was the largest

132
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:16,080
lifeboat, the James Caird.

133
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:17,120
The carpenter,

134
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:18,640
Harry McNish, raised

135
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:20,480
its sides, fitted a partial

136
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:22,080
covering of wood and canvas,

137
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:23,280
and worked to make

138
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:24,720
the hull more watertight.

139
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,880
Shackleton initially intended

140
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:28,160
to leave Thomas Crean

141
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:29,920
on Elephant Island to assist

142
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:31,120
his second-in-command,

143
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:32,080
Frank Wild.

144
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:33,840
But after discussing the plan,

145
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:35,120
he decided to take

146
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:36,080
Crean on the ocean

147
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:37,200
crossing instead.

148
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:39,680
On April 24, Shackleton

149
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:40,960
departed with Worsley,

150
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:42,560
McNish, Crean,

151
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:43,920
Timothy McCarthy

152
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:45,040
and John Vincent.

153
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,040
Twenty-two men remained

154
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:48,320
under Wild's command.

155
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:50,240
Wild's first challenge

156
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:51,440
was finding shelter.

157
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:53,040
An ice cave offered

158
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:54,240
protection from the wind,

159
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:55,440
but it was wet

160
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:56,960
and unsuitable for living in.

161
00:03:57,440 --> 00:03:58,640
The men abandoned it.

162
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:00,640
Instead, they built low

163
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:02,560
stone walls and used two

164
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:03,920
overturned lifeboats,

165
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:05,680
canvas and other salvaged

166
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,360
materials to form a roof.

167
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:09,040
The shelter was cramped,

168
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:09,760
but it gave

169
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:10,640
the men somewhere

170
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:12,400
to live as winter approached.

171
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:14,400
While Wild organised life

172
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:16,320
inside it, the James Caird

173
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,320
was already far out at sea.

174
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:20,160
Finding South Georgia

175
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:20,960
required more

176
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:22,320
than sailing northeast.

177
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:23,520
It was a small

178
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:25,600
island in an enormous ocean.

179
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,600
If the James Caird passed too

180
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:29,520
far to either side, the men

181
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:30,960
might miss it entirely.

182
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:32,720
Worsley had to navigate

183
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:33,600
with a sextant,

184
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:35,520
a compass and calculations

185
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,040
of their estimated progress.

186
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:38,480
He needed the sun

187
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:40,160
to determine their position.

188
00:04:40,267 --> 00:04:41,040
But cloud cover,

189
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,040
heavy seas and the constant

190
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:44,160
movement of the boat

191
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:46,080
made observations difficult.

192
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:47,200
During the crossing,

193
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:47,840
he obtained

194
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:49,840
only four usable observations

195
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:50,800
of the sun.

196
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:51,600
Between them,

197
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:53,040
he estimated their position

198
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:54,320
from the course steered

199
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:55,680
and distance travelled,

200
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:56,960
adjusting whenever new

201
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,240
evidence allowed.

202
00:04:58,400 --> 00:04:59,520
The others kept the boat

203
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:00,960
moving and afloat.

204
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:02,400
They worked in watches,

205
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:03,600
handling the sails,

206
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:05,600
steering and bailing water.

207
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:06,720
Ice accumulated

208
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,320
on the vessel, adding weight

209
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:10,000
that had to be removed.

210
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:12,160
After nearly two weeks at sea,

211
00:05:12,213 --> 00:05:13,040
they were approaching

212
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:14,480
where Worsley calculated

213
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:16,160
South Georgia should be.

214
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:17,680
On May 8, land

215
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:18,800
appeared through a break

216
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:20,000
in the clouds.

217
00:05:20,107 --> 00:05:21,520
They had found the island.

218
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:22,880
But heavy seas

219
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,240
and dangerous surf

220
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:24,800
prevented

221
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:26,000
an immediate landing.

222
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:27,520
For two more days,

223
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:28,160
they remained

224
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:29,600
offshore, searching

225
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:30,720
for a safe approach.

226
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:32,000
On May 10,

227
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:33,600
the six men finally

228
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,280
landed in King Haakon Bay,

229
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:36,560
on South Georgia's

230
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:37,440
western coast.

231
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:38,880
The whaling stations

232
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:39,680
were on the other

233
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:40,640
side of the island.

234
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:42,080
With the James Caird

235
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:42,800
and its crew

236
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:44,240
in their exhausted condition,

237
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:45,440
attempting to reach

238
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,800
those stations by sailing

239
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:47,680
around the coast

240
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:48,960
was too dangerous.

241
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:51,040
Shackleton decided to cross

242
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:52,320
the mountains instead.

243
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:54,080
McNish, Vincent

244
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:54,880
and McCarthy

245
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:56,000
remained behind.

246
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:57,840
Shackleton, Worsley

247
00:05:57,880 --> 00:05:59,600
and Crean set out on foot

248
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:00,800
on May 19.

249
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:02,240
There was no established

250
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:03,360
route through the mountains

251
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:04,160
and glaciers.

252
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,560
At one steep icy descent,

253
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:08,160
Worsley and Crean held

254
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:09,680
the rope while Shackleton

255
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:11,120
used a carpenter's adze

256
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:12,800
to cut steps into the ice.

257
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:14,160
Descending roughly

258
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:15,600
five hundred feet took

259
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:16,960
them about two hours.

260
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:18,640
Then the three continued

261
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:20,400
across snowfields and ridges,

262
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:21,920
searching for a passage

263
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,440
toward the inhabited coast.

264
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:24,800
On May 20,

265
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:26,000
after approximately

266
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:27,840
thirty-six hours of travelling,

267
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:29,040
they reached the whaling

268
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:30,480
station at Stromness.

269
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:31,920
The three men left

270
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:32,960
on South Georgia's

271
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:34,000
western shore

272
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:34,880
were subsequently

273
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:35,840
brought to safety.

274
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:37,120
But no news

275
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:38,720
of any of this had reached

276
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:39,600
Elephant Island.

277
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:41,120
Wild and the other

278
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:42,240
twenty-one men

279
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:43,280
were still waiting.

280
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,040
Inside their shelter,

281
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:46,240
they maintained a daily

282
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:47,920
routine, gathered food

283
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,440
when they could and endured

284
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:50,720
the Antarctic winter

285
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:51,760
without knowing whether

286
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,280
the James Caird had reached

287
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:54,640
its destination.

288
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:55,360
Wild kept

289
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:56,480
the group organised.

290
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:57,920
Each day, he rolled

291
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,360
up his own sleeping bag,

292
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:00,960
repeating that Shackleton

293
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,640
might arrive that day.

294
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:03,760
The others continued

295
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:04,800
the work of maintaining

296
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:06,880
the camp and finding food.

297
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:09,520
Days passed. Then weeks.

298
00:07:09,627 --> 00:07:10,880
Shackleton's first rescue

299
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:12,320
attempt, aboard the whaling

300
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:13,680
ship Southern Sky,

301
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:15,520
was stopped by pack ice.

302
00:07:15,627 --> 00:07:16,560
The next attempt,

303
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:18,080
using the Uruguayan vessel

304
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:20,240
Instituto de Pesca No. 1,

305
00:07:20,293 --> 00:07:21,200
and a later voyage

306
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:22,080
aboard the schooner

307
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:23,520
Emma also failed

308
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:25,200
to reach Elephant Island.

309
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:27,120
Three attempts had failed,

310
00:07:27,173 --> 00:07:28,400
and the men on the island

311
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:30,240
still had no news.

312
00:07:30,347 --> 00:07:32,000
As winter continued, food

313
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:34,080
became increasingly scarce.

314
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:35,520
By late August, the men

315
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:37,040
were using what remained

316
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:38,080
and gathering limpets

317
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:40,000
and seaweed along the shore.

318
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:41,200
Shackleton secured

319
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:42,880
a fourth opportunity.

320
00:07:42,987 --> 00:07:44,000
The Chilean government

321
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:45,840
provided Yelcho, a small

322
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:47,040
steam tug commanded

323
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:49,360
by naval officer Luis Pardo.

324
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:51,120
It was not an icebreaker.

325
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:52,400
The ship could not force

326
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:53,680
its way through a solid

327
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:55,280
barrier of pack ice.

328
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:56,800
On August 25,

329
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:57,920
Yelcho departed

330
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,120
with Shackleton aboard.

331
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:00,320
As the pack ice

332
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:01,920
shifted toward the northeast,

333
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:03,120
Shackleton chose

334
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:04,640
to approach Elephant Island

335
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:05,760
from the northwest.

336
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:07,520
Pardo then had to bring

337
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:08,640
the vessel safely

338
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:09,440
through the waters

339
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:10,240
near the island.

340
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:12,400
In fog, he navigated

341
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:13,840
among numerous grounded

342
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:15,760
icebergs, guiding the small

343
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:17,040
tug toward the camp.

344
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:19,600
On August 30, Yelcho

345
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:20,960
reached Elephant Island.

346
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:22,240
The men ashore

347
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:22,960
were preparing

348
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:24,640
food when a cry announced

349
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:25,600
the approaching ship.

350
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:28,240
At first, the words “Ship O!”

351
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:30,640
were mistaken for “Lunch O!”

352
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:33,280
Then the men saw the vessel.

353
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:34,880
After more than four

354
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:37,120
months of isolation, a ship

355
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:38,400
had reached their shore.

356
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:40,320
Pardo brought Yelcho close

357
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:41,680
enough for the evacuation

358
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:42,240
to begin.

359
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:43,920
All twenty-two men

360
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:44,720
who had remained

361
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:45,760
on Elephant Island

362
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:46,880
were taken aboard.

363
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:49,360
Every one of them was alive.

364
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:51,120
The wider Imperial

365
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:52,960
Trans-Antarctic Expedition

366
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:54,400
had a different outcome.

367
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:55,520
On the opposite

368
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:56,560
side of Antarctica,

369
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:58,560
the separate Ross Sea Party

370
00:08:58,600 --> 00:08:59,840
suffered its own ordeal,

371
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:01,600
and three men died.

372
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:02,560
The planned

373
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:04,080
crossing of Antarctica

374
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:05,360
was never completed.

375
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:06,960
But the twenty-two men

376
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:07,440
rescued

377
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:08,480
from Elephant Island,

378
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:09,840
together with the six

379
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:10,960
who had sailed for help,

380
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:12,880
accounted for every member

381
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:14,080
of the Endurance party.

382
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:16,400
The expedition lost its ship.

383
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:18,480
All twenty-eight men

384
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:19,840
came home alive.
